# Environment variables — NEVER commit real secrets!

# ─── LLM provider ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# JARVIS uses Gemini or Groq through the same OpenAI-compatible client.
# Set the key of ONE provider; JARVIS picks the provider whose key is set.
# Set JARVIS_LLM_PROVIDER=gemini or groq only if both keys are present.
# JARVIS_LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic: Claude answers (needs ANTHROPIC_API_KEY,
# see "Claude" below) and Gemini or Groq, if its key is set, is the fallback
# when a Claude call fails.

JARVIS_LLM_PROVIDER=

# ── Gemini (Google AI Studio key) ──

GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Optional. Defaults to the OpenAI-compatible endpoint below; the bare API root
# (https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta) also works.

GEMINI_API_URL=https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/openai

# ── Groq (https://console.groq.com) ──

GROQ_API_KEY=

# GitHub search and repository lookups (tools/githubTools.ts). Optional: without
# a token GitHub allows about 10 searches a minute. A read-only token is enough;
# JARVIS writes nothing to GitHub. Never paste the token into a file JARVIS can
# read (SEC-14) — keep it here only.

# GITHUB_TOKEN=
GROQ_API_URL=https://api.groq.com/openai/v1

# ── Models (must belong to the provider above) ──
# Gemini: gemini-3.5-flash and gemini-3.5-flash-lite (each has its own
#         free-tier rate limit, so using both spreads the load)
# Groq:   e.g. qwen/qwen3-32b and llama-3.1-8b-instant

JARVIS_BRAIN_MODEL=
JARVIS_FAST_MODEL=

# Thinking before answering: none | minimal | low | medium | high | off.
# Default for Gemini is minimal: thinking is paid out of max_tokens, and higher
# levels cut short replies off. "off" stops JARVIS sending the setting at all.
# Leave empty for Groq unless your model supports reasoning_effort.

JARVIS_LLM_REASONING_EFFORT=

# ─── Fallback LLM provider (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────

# Any OpenAI-compatible chat-completions endpoint, tried when the primary
# provider fails. Without this, a Groq outage stops all reasoning. Leave the key
# empty and set only the base URL for a local server that needs no auth.

JARVIS_FALLBACK_BASE_URL=
JARVIS_FALLBACK_API_KEY=
JARVIS_FALLBACK_MODEL=

# ─── Model per kind of work (optional; bridge/modelCategories.ts) ─────────────

# Each kind of work uses the brain or fast model above unless set here:
#   <model>                same provider, another model
#   <provider>:<model>     e.g. anthropic:claude-opus-5-5
#   anthropic              Claude with its default model (claude-opus-5-5)
# Kinds: INTENT, CONVERSATION, PLANNING, REPAIR_PLAN, CODING, REVIEW.
# JARVIS_MODEL_CODING=
# JARVIS_MODEL_REVIEW=
# JARVIS_MODEL_CODING_TIMEOUT_MS=300000
# A kind set here is sent to that provider and model.
#   Strict: CODING and REVIEW (code repair, and the goal checks, which ask as
#   REVIEW), and the two agent settings below. They go to that one provider
#   and model, even when only a model name is given (then: the provider
#   above). If it fails, that call fails with that reason; no other provider
#   or model answers it. On a strict route the provider must also answer with
#   exactly that model: the Gemini/Groq client does not move to its fast
#   model, Claude's server-side fallback models are off, and a cached reply of
#   a non-strict call is not reused (a cached strict reply is checked again). The answer is accepted only when the provider
#   reports the model it used and that is the configured one (Claude: or the
#   dated id its model list gives for it); no report, or another model, and
#   the call fails ("model identity not confirmed"). A strict route is not
#   streamed, so no text appears before that check. A strict route that would
#   be sent as another model is refused before sending: "openai:<model>" for a
#   model other than JARVIS_FALLBACK_MODEL (that provider always sends its
#   own), and, with Claude as the primary, a bare non-Claude model name (it
#   would be sent as a Claude model). Live providers may report a model id in
#   another form than the one configured (e.g. a dated id for an alias): such
#   a strict route then fails until the configured name matches what the
#   provider reports; check this on the PC with the real providers.
#   Not strict: INTENT, CONVERSATION, PLANNING, REPAIR_PLAN. If the set
#   provider and model fail, the failover order above answers with the
#   model the caller asked for. A provider may be asked a second time, with
#   a different model, only when the first failure was about the model
#   (e.g. 404 "model not found", a refused request). After a sign-in,
#   network, rate-limit, server or time-out failure, or an open breaker,
#   that provider is not asked again in that call. No provider and model
#   pair is asked twice; when all have failed, the error says so.
# A time limit or token cap applies only when set. The time limit applies to
# the set provider's own attempt and counts from when it is asked, not while
# a background call waits for you.
# One specialist agent's own model (same syntax), e.g. QA on another model
# than coding:
# JARVIS_MODEL_AGENT_CODING_AGENT=
# JARVIS_MODEL_AGENT_QA_AGENT=
# What a task result means:
#   - An agent task that ends COMPLETED with data.answeredBy = "model" was
#     answered by its model. "keyword_rules" means its model failed or gave
#     no answer and fixed keyword rules ran read-only tools instead. That is
#     not an AI answer. It can happen for every agent except QA and a
#     coding_agent with its own setting: only those two settings are strict,
#     so e.g. a browser agent with its own setting can still end this way.
#   - A coding_agent with its own setting ends FAILED, with the reason, when
#     its model fails, gives an empty answer or its model-call budget is used
#     up. When it splits a request into parts, every helper agent uses that
#     same model and strict routing (never the PLANNING setting or keyword
#     rules), and the coding task ends FAILED if any part failed or was not
#     answered by that model. Every agent below it (helpers, research
#     workers, their workers) uses that same model; this follows who spawned
#     whom, never task input. Its GitHub research flow keeps its workers, but
#     a model failure or an unusable answer anywhere in it ends the task
#     FAILED (no rules, no retry of the failed worker). The same holds for QA
#     with its own setting and the agents below it. Agents without their own
#     setting keep their own route and fallback, as before.
#   - QA's own work (reviews, "run the type check") never falls back to
#     keyword rules: if its model fails, set or not, gives an empty answer,
#     or any part (a helper's or its own) was not completed by its model,
#     the QA task ends FAILED with the reason.
#   - QA's automatic check of another agent's result uses fixed rules and no
#     model; it is not an AI verification. "Verified" means every check
#     passed. A check that could not run (a cited source not re-read: the
#     site unreachable, the worker not finished or not allowed, no licence to
#     compare) makes it "inconclusive": not verified, with the full reason,
#     and it needs your review. A goal's "verified" criterion is met only by
#     results that were verified; anything else waits for review. A goal
#     task whose verification is inconclusive, or did not finish, pauses the
#     goal for your review (VERIFICATION_INCONCLUSIVE). An agent result goes
#     into long-term memory only when the Verification agent verified it:
#     with JARVIS_AGENT_VERIFY=0 nothing is stored. Browser, PC and memory
#     results are verified only when they have conflicts; QA results never
#     are. The full result is still reported, and kept in the task archive
#     (data/agents/<task>.json) unless JARVIS_AGENT_ARCHIVE=0. In a goal, a
#     result a later step builds on is marked "not verified" when it was not
#     verified. save_relation keeps only what you confirm: during a request
#     (yours, or an agent's task) it shows you the fact, naming the agent that
#     asks, and stores it only on "yes", given within 20 seconds. Within one
#     agent task, or one of your requests answered by JARVIS itself, a fact
#     asked about one call at a time is asked once: a "no" or no answer is
#     not asked again, a "yes" stores it once, and a store that failed is tried
#     again without asking. Two calls for the same fact at the same moment, or
#     JARVIS and an agent it started in the same request, are asked
#     separately. The question does not count against the tool's time limit,
#     and a request that is stopped withdraws it and stores nothing. Refused
#     without asking: a fact longer than 150 characters; one with a line
#     break, a control character, a run of spaces, or a character the screen
#     does not show (format and default-ignorable characters, the blank
#     Braille pattern); and a password, key or token, also when split across
#     the parts. This also refuses some ordinary text: Persian or Urdu typed
#     with a zero-width non-joiner, scripts or emoji joined with a zero-width
#     joiner, keycaps and subdivision flags. A single emoji with its own
#     variation selector (U+FE0F right after it) is allowed. By voice, a fact
#     longer than about 56 characters, or with markdown that speech leaves
#     out, needs a typed answer. Inside a goal nothing is stored and nobody is
#     asked; a goal step that tried to keep a fact, or whose text starts
#     (after an optional "First,", "Then" or "Next,") by asking to keep one
#     in English (remember that, store/save/record that, memorize, make a
#     note, keep track of, log, write down, persist, or "remember who/when/
#     where …: value"), or to put it in memory, or to update the profile or
#     preferences, waits for your review. Other wordings, other languages and
#     later requests in the step are not read; "Note that …" can be held for
#     review although it stores nothing. forget_memory removes a kept fact; it
#     does not remove a graph-memory edge, or the note in your second-brain
#     vault when a vault is configured.
#   - A split request whose parts failed for different reasons keeps every
#     cause; recovery acts on the identity failure first, then a model that
#     did not answer, and waits for budget only when every part ran out of
#     budget.
#   - Independent QA (QA on a model distinct from every model coding can be
#     answered by, failover included, and the models of its helpers that have
#     their own JARVIS_MODEL_AGENT_<HELPER ROLE> setting). Counted as the same
#     model: names that reduce to the same model id (case, a "models/" or
#     gateway "vendor/" prefix, a Bedrock "vendor." or "region.vendor."
#     prefix, a ":variant" suffix, a date stamp, a "-001" version, a "-v2"
#     version after a number, "5.5" = "5-5"); two ids the provider treats as
#     one model (Claude: an alias and the dated id its model list returns); a
#     model whose identity cannot be looked up, or a floating alias
#     ("...-latest", ":latest"); two models on one
#     Gemini/Groq client, or two Claude models, unless both are strict routes.
#     Identities are looked up only when independence is required, each look-
#     up limited to JARVIS_QA_IDENTITY_LOOKUP_MS (default 10000, 1000-60000);
#     a failed or timed-out look-up counts as unknown and is not retried for
#     a minute. Only these name forms are recognised: do not give QA another
#     provider's id for coding's model.
#       JARVIS_QA_REQUIRE_INDEPENDENT=true   required
#       JARVIS_QA_REQUIRE_INDEPENDENT=false  not required; QA runs and its
#                                            result is labelled not independent
#       unset                                required when QA has its own
#                                            setting (JARVIS_MODEL_AGENT_QA_AGENT)
#     When required, QA work (QA and the agents below it) ends FAILED
#     "independent QA unavailable … human review required" before any model
#     is asked if no distinct model is available, and after a reply that does
#     not report its model or reports one coding may use. A QA result carries
#     data.independence: "independent" only when required and every reply
#     reported a distinct model; never when not required.
# JARVIS_QA_REQUIRE_INDEPENDENT=
# JARVIS_QA_IDENTITY_LOOKUP_MS=10000
# Failure codes an agent task or goal records (they stay distinct):
#   MODEL_UNAVAILABLE           the required model did not answer: the goal
#                               retries later, waiting for the service
#   MODEL_IDENTITY_UNCONFIRMED  the provider did not confirm the configured
#                               model: the goal is blocked until the setting
#                               is fixed (not retried)
#   TOOL_BUDGET_EXHAUSTED       the tool-call budget ran out (the agent task
#                               shows BUDGET_EXCEEDED with budget "toolCalls"):
#                               the goal waits for budget; never a model failure
#   VERIFICATION_INCONCLUSIVE   a verification check did not happen: the goal
#                               waits for your review
# A background call (agents, goals) waits while you are talking to JARVIS,
# before the set provider and before each fallback attempt, at most
# JARVIS_BACKGROUND_YIELD_MS each time (default 20000, at most 120000), then
# goes ahead. Your own calls never wait. To fail such a call instead when one
# wait reaches this long (a clear error; it is not sent, and no other model
# answers it):
# JARVIS_MODEL_QUEUE_WAIT_MS=
# This limit covers calls for a kind or agent set above (and their fallback
# attempts) and code repair's coding and review calls, set or not; other
# background calls wait as before and then go ahead.
#
# Each provider has its own rate-limit breaker (2 rate limits pause it for
# 60 s; failover answers meanwhile; then one trial call decides) and an
# optional limit of requests a minute, e.g. 5 for the Gemini free tier.
# Gemini and Groq use their client's own breaker (bridge/groqProvider.ts);
# the two BREAKER settings below apply to ANTHROPIC and OPENAI only. The
# request limit applies to all four. This breaker is new: before Phase 1A,
# Claude and the OpenAI-compatible fallback were asked after every 429.
# JARVIS_PROVIDER_<NAME>_RPM=              (<NAME>: GEMINI, GROQ, ANTHROPIC, OPENAI)
# JARVIS_PROVIDER_<NAME>_BREAKER_429S=2    (<NAME>: ANTHROPIC, OPENAI)
# JARVIS_PROVIDER_<NAME>_BREAKER_MS=60000  (<NAME>: ANTHROPIC, OPENAI)
# Usage records are kept in data/usage/ (no message text); off turns that off:
# JARVIS_USAGE_LEDGER=off
# Price per million tokens, only to estimate cost where token counts are known:
# JARVIS_MODEL_PRICES={"claude-opus-5-5":{"input":4,"output":20}}

# ─── Claude (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# With JARVIS_LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic, Claude answers every request. Otherwise
# it is used only by a kind of work that names "anthropic" above, and never as
# a fallback for ordinary requests. The model is checked against your key's
# model list before its first use.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# JARVIS_ANTHROPIC_BRAIN_MODEL=claude-opus-5-5
# JARVIS_ANTHROPIC_FAST_MODEL=claude-haiku-5-5
# Only for a proxy or gateway in front of the Claude API:
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=
# JARVIS_ANTHROPIC_EFFORT=high
# Server-side fallback when Claude declines a request (on by default):
# JARVIS_ANTHROPIC_FALLBACKS=off

# ─── Self-repair limits (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────────

# "heal yourself" and "propose a code repair": attempts per run, minutes per
# run, and the model-token cap for a code repair.
# JARVIS_REPAIR_MAX_ATTEMPTS=2
# JARVIS_REPAIR_MAX_MINUTES=20
# JARVIS_REPAIR_TOKEN_CAP=200000
# JARVIS's second brain (memory/secondBrain.ts), off by default. The full path
# of your Obsidian vault: the folder that holds a .obsidian folder. JARVIS only
# writes under <vault>/JARVIS/ and never changes, moves or deletes your notes.
# Example (Windows): JARVIS_OBSIDIAN_VAULT=C:\Users\you\Documents\MyVault
# Check it (writes, reads, finds and removes one test note): pnpm check:brain
# JARVIS_OBSIDIAN_VAULT=

# ─── Workspace containment ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Absolute path to the JARVIS project root. This is the containment boundary for
# all agent file I/O (read_file / write_file / control_file / run_command cwd).
# Leave empty to derive it from the installed location, which is correct for a
# normal checkout. Set it explicitly only if the project is launched from a
# different directory than it lives in.

JARVIS_WORKSPACE_ROOT=

# ─── Permissions ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# What JARVIS may do without asking (docs/upgrade/PERMISSION_MODEL.md).
# 1 (default): reading the PC and low-risk actions (open an app or a web page,
# focus a window) run at once. 0: the previous default, where control_app and
# control_window need full control mode as well.
# JARVIS_DEFAULT_PERMISSION_LEVEL=1
#
# Actions of level 2 and above (type, click, close an app, write a file, end a
# process): session (default) = only while full control mode is on, and level 3
# and 4 are also asked each time; ask = JARVIS asks you to approve each one,
# with or without full control mode.
# JARVIS_LEVEL2_POLICY=session
#
# Highest risk level a spoken "yes" may approve (0-3, default 2). Above it the
# request must be approved by typing in the console; voice can still cancel.
# The microphone can pick up a video, a call or another person.
# JARVIS_VOICE_APPROVAL_MAX_RISK=2
#
# How long a typed approval waits for your answer, in seconds (10–120; default
# 30). No answer still denies the action. Three ways to give it 120 s:
#   - here in .env (every run):           JARVIS_APPROVAL_TIMEOUT_S=120
#   - CMD, this window only:              set JARVIS_APPROVAL_TIMEOUT_S=120
#   - PowerShell, this window only:       $env:JARVIS_APPROVAL_TIMEOUT_S = "120"
# In CMD, put no spaces around "=" and no quotes.
# JARVIS_APPROVAL_TIMEOUT_S=30
#
# Most times each tool may run per minute, at risk level 0,1,2,3,4 (0 = no limit).
# JARVIS_TOOL_RATE_LIMITS=120,60,20,10,10
#
# While you talk to JARVIS, background agents and goals wait this long (ms) at
# most before each model call, so your request gets the provider quota first.
# 0 = do not wait.
# JARVIS_BACKGROUND_YIELD_MS=20000
#
# The local Control Center: a page at http://127.0.0.1:7790 on this PC only.
# Log in with the token in data\security\control_center_token (made at the
# first start), or set your own below. Actions use the same permission checks
# as a spoken request; turning on full control is still approved in the console.
# JARVIS_CONTROL_CENTER=1
# JARVIS_CONTROL_CENTER_PORT=7790
# JARVIS_CONTROL_CENTER_TOKEN=

# ─── Development awareness ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Local ports "is my backend running?" checks (127.0.0.1 only).
# JARVIS_DEV_PORTS=3000,3001,4200,5000,5173,8000,8080,8081,8888
#
# Folders whose git repositories JARVIS may read (;-separated; it looks at each
# folder and one level below). Default: the JARVIS folder.
# JARVIS_PROJECT_DIRS=C:\Users\you\code;D:\work
#
# How long a test/build script may run, and how long a dev server has to
# answer on its port (milliseconds).
# JARVIS_DEV_RUN_TIMEOUT_MS=600000
# JARVIS_DEV_SERVER_START_MS=30000

# ─── Browser ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# JARVIS opens pages in a Chrome of its own, which it starts when needed with
# a separate profile and a free debugging port; it does not open pages in, or
# close, your everyday Chrome. "Close the browser" closes only JARVIS's Chrome,
# after asking. That Chrome keeps the sign-ins you make in it and stays open
# when JARVIS exits; its debugging port listens on 127.0.0.1 only, without a
# password, so other programs on this PC can use it too.
# Leave these unset unless you need them:
# Chrome's program, if it is not in the standard install folder:
# JARVIS_CHROME_PATH=C:\Program Files\Google\Chrome\Application\chrome.exe
# The profile folder (default %USERPROFILE%\jarvis-chrome-profile). If you
# used another folder for JARVIS's Chrome before (earlier instructions named
# W:\jarvis-chrome-profile), set it here to keep that profile's sign-ins:
# JARVIS_CHROME_PROFILE=C:\Users\you\jarvis-chrome-profile
# off: JARVIS never starts Chrome itself.
# JARVIS_CHROME_AUTOSTART=off
#
# Only if you start the Chrome yourself: its debugging port. When this is set,
# JARVIS uses that Chrome and starts none.
#   start chrome.exe --remote-debugging-port=9222 --user-data-dir="%USERPROFILE%\jarvis-chrome-profile"
# JARVIS_CDP_PORT=9222
#
# Where browser downloads go (an absolute path). Default: Downloads\jarvis in
# your user folder. Downloaded files are never opened by JARVIS.
# JARVIS_DOWNLOAD_DIR=C:\Users\you\Downloads\jarvis

# ─── Runtime tuning for older local PCs ───────────────────────────────────────

JARVIS_LLM_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
# Leave empty: planning then waits for every retry of a model request (3 × the timeout above + 2 s).
JARVIS_PLANNING_WATCHDOG_MS=
JARVIS_MEMORY_WRITE_DEBOUNCE_MS=2000
JARVIS_ACTIVE_WINDOW_POLL_MS=4000
JARVIS_OPEN_APPS_POLL_MS=5000
JARVIS_CHROME_TABS_POLL_MS=7000
JARVIS_SERVICES_POLL_MS=15000
JARVIS_SYSTEM_STATS_POLL_MS=10000
JARVIS_SYSTEM_STATE_WRITE_MS=3000

# A command heard while JARVIS is busy waits at most this long (ms) before it
# is skipped; JARVIS says when it skips one.
# JARVIS_QUEUED_COMMAND_MAX_AGE_MS=30000

# Seconds after a reply in which a follow-up needs no wake word (0 = off).
# JARVIS_FOLLOWUP_SECONDS=15

# off = do not start the screen-capture (Vision) service. Nothing switches it
# on yet, so on a low-memory PC it only holds memory.
# JARVIS_VISION=off

# ─── Vector memory model download (optional) ─────────────────────────────────

# The embedding model is downloaded from Hugging Face once, then loaded from the
# local cache. A free read token (huggingface.co/settings/tokens) lifts the
# anonymous rate limit on that first download.

# HF_TOKEN=

# ─── Vector memory persistence ────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Where the vector store is written. Defaults to <project>/data/vector.

JARVIS_VECTOR_STORE_DIR=

# Set false to run the vector store in RAM only (previous behaviour).

JARVIS_VECTOR_PERSIST=true

# Flush the store to disk after this many accepted writes.

JARVIS_VECTOR_PERSIST_EVERY=10

# The vector service (127.0.0.1:8000) answers only requests carrying this token.
# Leave empty: JARVIS makes one and keeps it in data/security/vector_token, which
# the service reads too. Set it only if you start vectorMemory.py yourself with
# a different data folder.

JARVIS_VECTOR_TOKEN=

# Cap on how many facts are re-embedded when no persisted store is found.
# Empty or 0 means all of them. (This used to be hard-coded to 10.)

JARVIS_VECTOR_REBUILD_LIMIT=

# Minimum gap in ms between two calls to the same tool.

JARVIS_MIN_TOOL_GAP_MS=250

# ─── xAI ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Not used by JARVIS: no code calls xAI. Kept only so older .env files still
# parse. It does not count as an LLM key.

XAI_API_KEY=

# ─── Graph memory (Neo4j — optional, off by default) ─────────────────────────

# Master switch. Graph memory stays disabled unless this is exactly "true".

JARVIS_NEO4J_ENABLED=false
NEO4J_URI=bolt://localhost:7687
NEO4J_USER=neo4j

# Required when JARVIS_NEO4J_ENABLED=true. There is no default: the driver
# refuses to connect without it rather than trying a well-known password.

NEO4J_PASSWORD=

# ─── Voice ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

TTS_VOICE=en-US-GuyNeural

# Wake-word log detail: INFO (default) or DEBUG.

JARVIS_WAKEWORD_LOG_LEVEL=

# ─── Local Python Bridge ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

BRIDGE_HOST=127.0.0.1
BRIDGE_PORT=9000

# Required shared secret for local Python voice clients.

# Set your own strong secret in the real .env file.

JARVIS_BRIDGE_TOKEN=

# Set true ONLY if you explicitly want local insecure development without a token.
# Dev mode skips the token only: a web page (any browser Origin) is refused in
# every mode, so it can never send commands or answer approvals.

JARVIS_BRIDGE_DEV_MODE=false

# Browser origins allowed to connect to the bridge, comma-separated and exact
# (e.g. http://127.0.0.1:5555). Leave empty: JARVIS's own clients send none.

JARVIS_BRIDGE_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# Largest message a bridge client may send, in MB (default 16; vision frames are the largest).

JARVIS_BRIDGE_MAX_PAYLOAD_MB=

# Full URI used by Python voice modules

BRIDGE_WS_URI=ws://127.0.0.1:9000

# ─── Slow PC or slow connection (all optional) ───────────────────────────────

# Time budget for one tool call by its risk, in ms (defaults 30000 / 20000 / 15000).
# When it runs out the tool's process and everything it started are stopped,
# the call ends as a timeout, and it is not retried.

JARVIS_TOOL_BUDGET_LOW_MS=
JARVIS_TOOL_BUDGET_MEDIUM_MS=
JARVIS_TOOL_BUDGET_HIGH_MS=

# How long a stopped tool gets to end before JARVIS stops waiting for it (default 5000).

JARVIS_TOOL_STOP_GRACE_MS=

# One LLM request may take this long before it is cancelled (default 10000).

# JARVIS_LLM_TIMEOUT_MS=20000

# Planning gives up after this long. Default: 3 × JARVIS_LLM_TIMEOUT_MS + 2000 (32000),
# so the model's own retries can finish. Best left empty: the default follows the
# timeout above. With JARVIS_LLM_TIMEOUT_MS=20000 that is 3 × 20000 + 2000 = 62000.
# A lower value is used as set, with a warning (it cuts slow answers off); a value
# that is not a positive number of milliseconds is not used, also with a warning.

# JARVIS_PLANNING_WATCHDOG_MS=62000

# Background checks: active window, Chrome tabs, JARVIS services (defaults 8000, 12000, 20000).

# JARVIS_ACTIVE_WINDOW_POLL_MS=20000

# JARVIS_CHROME_TABS_POLL_MS=60000

# JARVIS_SERVICES_POLL_MS=60000

# Python services print at once, so the log shows their loading times.

# PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1

# A listen session nobody ends returns to idle after this long, and the mic
# goes back to the wake word (default 35000).

# JARVIS_LISTENING_WATCHDOG_MS=35000

# A command spoken while Whisper is still loading waits this many seconds,
# then Google STT transcribes it (default 5).

# JARVIS_STT_MODEL_WAIT_S=5

# The STT noise floor of a session may be at most this many times the quiet
# room's (default 2.0), so speech in the calibration sample does not raise it.

# JARVIS_STT_MAX_FLOOR_RISE=2.0

# After edge-tts refuses (HTTP 403) or fails twice in a row, the offline voice is used
# for this many seconds before edge-tts is tried again (default 600).

# JARVIS_TTS_EDGE_SKIP_S=600

# How long a memory search waits for the vector service before using keyword
# search, in ms (default 1500).

# JARVIS_VECTOR_SEARCH_BUDGET_MS=1500

# Which alerts are spoken: critical (default), warning, or off. All are logged.

# JARVIS_VOICE_ALERT_LEVEL=critical

# A garbage-collection pause longer than this (ms) raises a warning (default 500).

# JARVIS_GC_WARN_MS=500

# ─── Tests only (pnpm test) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# windowsScriptsTest checks JARVIS's PowerShell files with PowerShell 7 (pwsh).
# JARVIS itself keeps running on Windows PowerShell 5.1; this is only for the
# test. Install it in CMD:   winget install --id Microsoft.PowerShell --source winget
# then open a NEW CMD window and check:   pwsh -v
# The test finds pwsh on the PATH or in C:\Program Files\PowerShell\7. Elsewhere,
# name it in the window that runs the tests (the runner does not read it from
# this file), for example in CMD:
#   set JARVIS_TEST_PWSH=D:\Tools\PowerShell\7\pwsh.exe
